WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.775 --> 00:00:12.155
practiced with the Long Noses.

2
00:00:13.355 --> 00:00:18.377
The performers wore costumes from the Middle Ages, and each man had a splendid pair of wings on

3
00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:21.078
his shoulders and a very long nose attached to his face.

4
00:00:22.198 --> 00:00:25.199
The noses were made of bamboo and some were ten feet long!

5
00:00:26.399 --> 00:00:29.420
Passepartout was a little embarrassed to be in a circus act again.

6
00:00:30.556 --> 00:00:32.217
It reminded him too much of his youth.

7
00:00:33.237 --> 00:00:37.079
But he cheered up, remembering his long nose was helping him to earn money for food.

8
00:00:37.779 --> 00:00:40.580
He went onstage and took his place with the rest of the performers.

9
00:00:41.821 --> 00:00:45.642
They balanced chairs, balls, and plates on the ends of their long noses.

10
00:00:47.063 --> 00:00:52.265
Some climbed up to stand on their fellow performers' noses, performing handstands and acrobatic flips.

11
00:00:53.205 --> 00:00:56.486
The theater was full, and the audience was delighted by the spectacle.

12
00:00:57.347 --> 00:00:59.888
The grand finale was a feat called the "Great Pyramid."

13
00:01:00.924 --> 00:01:04.245
A group of acrobats lay on the floor with their noses pointed at the ceiling.

14
00:01:05.685 --> 00:01:09.806
A second group lay on the tips of the noses, and a third group climbed up on the

15
00:01:09.846 --> 00:01:10.987
noses of the second group.

16
00:01:11.987 --> 00:01:15.448
A fourth group did the same, and a towering human pyramid was formed.

17
00:01:16.428 --> 00:01:21.329
There was loud applause, and the orchestra began its fanfare when the pyramid began to tip from side

18
00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:21.830
to side.

19
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.331
One of the lower noses had vanished!

20
00:01:27.431 --> 00:01:30.552
The pyramid tilted, tumbled, and collapsed like a house of cards.

21
00:01:32.844 --> 00:01:34.785
The missing performer was Passepartout.

22
00:01:34.805 --> 00:01:39.868
"Master!" he cried as he jumped into the crowd and fell at the feet of one of the

23
00:01:39.908 --> 00:01:41.890
spectators. Mr.

24
00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:46.552
Fogg and Aouda, who were seated in the audience, were shocked— as if they had been struck by

25
00:01:46.612 --> 00:01:51.135
lightning. Passepartout was kneeling before them in his performer's costume.

26
00:01:51.155 --> 00:01:54.777
"Passepartout! We are so happy to find you!"

27
00:01:55.138 --> 00:01:55.778
cried Aouda.

28
00:01:57.339 --> 00:01:58.720
Mr. Batulcar was furious.

29
00:01:59.692 --> 00:02:02.653
He demanded compensation for his ruined show, and Mr.

30
00:02:02.673 --> 00:02:04.913
Fogg happily appeased him with a handful of banknotes.

31
00:02:06.574 --> 00:02:09.715
The three traveling companions left the theater, together once again.

32
00:02:10.655 --> 00:02:15.856
As they made their way through the streets of Yokohama, Aouda explained to Passepartout how they had finally

33
00:02:15.896 --> 00:02:16.316
found him.

34
00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:20.978
"We reached Yokohama on the morning of November 14," she said.

35
00:02:20.998 --> 00:02:22.258
"And Mr.

36
00:02:22.298 --> 00:02:25.899
Fogg immediately went on board the Carnatic, which was still docked in the harbor.

37
00:02:27.059 --> 00:02:30.424
He learned, to our delight, that you had been onboard."

38
00:02:30.444 --> 00:02:34.106
"Yes. Luckily, I did not miss the steamer," said Passepartout.

39
00:02:34.126 --> 00:02:39.389
"So we knew that you had arrived only the day before," Aouda continued.

40
00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:43.591
"We visited the French and English consuls, but they had received no word of you.

41
00:02:44.472 --> 00:02:46.953
After wandering through the streets, we began to despair.

42
00:02:48.094 --> 00:02:49.054
Chance led us to Mr.

43
00:02:49.074 --> 00:02:49.835
Batulcar's theater.

44
00:02:51.075 --> 00:02:54.317
We did not recognize you in your costume, but fortunately, you saw us!"

45
00:02:56.278 --> 00:02:59.160
"You cannot imagine, madam, how happy I am to be back with you!"

46
00:02:59.584 --> 00:03:01.405
said Passepartout, smiling widely.

47
00:03:02.186 --> 00:03:03.006
He turned to Mr.

48
00:03:03.046 --> 00:03:04.267
Fogg with a serious face.

49
00:03:05.127 --> 00:03:07.929
"Sir, please forgive my poor behavior," he said.

50
00:03:07.949 --> 00:03:10.771
"It was because of me that you missed the Carnatic.

51
00:03:11.411 --> 00:03:12.331
I beg your forgiveness.

52
00:03:13.172 --> 00:03:15.693
I was drunk and did not tell you of the schedule change.

53
00:03:17.274 --> 00:03:18.375
It will never happen again.

54
00:03:18.975 --> 00:03:19.876
Please believe me!"

55
00:03:19.896 --> 00:03:22.477
"You are forgiven," said Mr.

56
00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:24.839
Fogg. "We will talk no more about it!"

57
00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:26.740
They walked on in silence.

58
00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.941
Passepartout was relieved to be back with his master.

59
00:03:29.961 --> 00:03:33.242
Aouda was very happy to see her friend once again.

60
00:03:34.062 --> 00:03:37.243
Mr. Fogg was his usual self, as calm and distant as ever.

61
00:03:38.663 --> 00:03:40.944
Passepartout then realized that, although Mr.

62
00:03:40.964 --> 00:03:45.845
Fogg and Aouda had missed the Carnatic, somehow they had arrived in Yokohama only one day after him!

63
00:03:47.206 --> 00:03:49.086
He turned to Aouda for an explanation.

64
00:03:50.007 --> 00:03:54.108
She described their search for a boat in the port at Hong Kong and the perilous voyage they

65
00:03:54.128 --> 00:03:54.708
had survived.

66
00:03:55.592 --> 00:03:57.833
Aouda then explained what had happened at Shanghai.

67
00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:03.534
The Yokohama steamer was already underway as they approached the harbor.

68
00:04:05.135 --> 00:04:05.495
At Mr.

69
00:04:05.535 --> 00:04:08.516
Fogg's request, the Tankadere signaled the steamer with its whistle.

70
00:04:10.656 --> 00:04:16.038
The captain of the steamer heard the signal and saw the Tankadere's flag at half-mast, which indicates a

71
00:04:16.098 --> 00:04:16.878
ship in distress.

72
00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:19.159
Mr. Fogg had arranged this.

73
00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:22.340
The steamer directed its course to the little boat.

74
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:29.364
Phileas Fogg quickly paid Captain Bunsby their agreed price, rewarding him with an additional 550 pounds.

75
00:04:30.244 --> 00:04:32.106
The steamer let down a ladder, and Mr.

76
00:04:32.146 --> 00:04:34.567
Fogg and Aouda climbed aboard, followed by Mr.

77
00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.648
Fix. Mr.

78
00:04:36.668 --> 00:04:40.151
Fogg listened politely as Aouda spoke, without adding a word of his own.

79
00:04:43.092 --> 00:04:47.695
She finished her story as they reached the steamer, which was now docked in Yokohama harbor and bound

80
00:04:47.735 --> 00:04:48.576
for San Francisco.

81
00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:54.640
Mr. Fogg then gave Passepartout the necessary funds to buy clothes that better suited his position.

82
00:04:56.121 --> 00:05:01.245
Within an hour Passepartout had cut off his long nose, parted with his wings, and looked like his

83
00:05:01.285 --> 00:05:02.746
master's servant once more.

84
00:05:08.711 --> 00:05:10.132
That was a neat little trick that Mr.

85
00:05:10.152 --> 00:05:11.173
Fogg arranged, wasn't it?

86
00:05:11.994 --> 00:05:14.015
Putting the ship's flag at half-mast, I mean.

87
00:05:14.876 --> 00:05:17.218
In the days before radio, how did ships communicate?

88
00:05:18.259 --> 00:05:19.420
Did they yell really loudly?

89
00:05:20.336 --> 00:05:22.477
Ha-ha! No, they used flags.

90
00:05:23.137 --> 00:05:24.797
Flags are very important at sea.

91
00:05:25.538 --> 00:05:27.578
They can mean the difference between life and death!

92
00:05:28.599 --> 00:05:32.840
Flags are flown when a ship is entering, or leaving, a harbor, and when a ship is sailing

93
00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:33.680
in foreign waters.

94
00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.822
But why did the ship, the Tankadere, fly its flag only halfway up the mast?

95
00:05:40.082 --> 00:05:40.902
Was the flag broken?

96
00:05:41.782 --> 00:05:42.142
No no!

97
00:05:42.683 --> 00:05:45.323
Flying a flag halfway like that is called "half-mast."

98
00:05:45.664 --> 00:05:47.024
It means the ship is in trouble.

99
00:05:48.368 --> 00:05:51.392
On land a flag at half-mast means that someone important has died.

100
00:05:53.013 --> 00:05:56.217
There are special days when a country's flag is flown at half-mast too.

101
00:05:57.398 --> 00:05:58.539
Why did this tradition develop?

102
00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.606
Well, people decided that if a flag is halfway down, there is room for another "invisible" flag at

103
00:06:04.626 --> 00:06:05.747
the top of the flagpole.

104
00:06:06.388 --> 00:06:09.812
This "invisible" flag represents and honors the person who has died.
